---
title: Flags & experiments
summary: Kill switches, rollouts, targeting, and A/B tests as ONE primitive, flag(). Decided per visitor, server-side during SSR, with zero JS for the decision. OpenFeature connects through one decide() call. csr=false pages A/B test with no extra cost.
related:
  - app/server-router
  - app/federation
  - regions/timing-nesting
---

A kill switch, a staged rollout, a targeting rule, and an A/B/n test are the same thing: **a name with variants, decided per visitor**. One primitive owns that decision: `flag()`. The decision occurs **server-side during SSR**. Thus the selected variant is simply what renders. There is no flicker, no client SDK, and no JS for the decision. A `csr = false` page A/B tests with no extra cost.

You read a flag when you **call it**. You branch with `pick`. You connect a vendor (OpenFeature) with one `decide()` call. That is the full surface.

## Declare — the second argument is the shape

```ts
import { flag } from 'ogygia/flag';

export const checkout = flag('new-checkout');                    // kill switch — off until decided on
export const promo    = flag('summer-promo', 10);                // number → sticky rollout to 10%
export const beta     = flag('beta', (c) => !!c.visitor?.beta);  // function → targeting
export const hero     = flag('hero', { control: 80, bold: 20 }); // record → weighted variants (ratios)
```

- **omitted** → a kill switch, off for all visitors. This is the safe default.
- **a number** → the sticky percent of traffic that gets the feature.
- **a function** → per-request targeting. `true` = on. `false` = off, and a rollout cannot include the visitor again. `undefined` = fall through. It runs on each request. Thus you can read a cookie, an env var, or a DB row: runtime toggles without a redeploy.
- **a record** → weighted variants. Weights are **ratios** (`{ control: 3, bold: 1 }` is 75/25). The first key is control.

## Read — by calling the flag

```ts
'/': page(Home, {
  load: (c) => ({
    dark: checkout(c),        // boolean
    variant: hero(c),         // 'control' | 'bold' — sticky per visitor
  }),
}),
```

The decision is **deterministic and sticky**. The same visitor gets the same answer on each request, from a hash of `(flag name, visitor id)`. There is no storage, no cookie write, and nothing to sync.

## Branch — `pick`, one verb, two forms

`pick` means: "here is a map of variant → thing; give this visitor their entry."

**A page slot** (no `c` — the router supplies it per request):

```ts
import Hero from './Hero.svelte';
import HeroBold from './HeroBold.svelte';

'/': page(hero.pick({ control: Hero, bold: HeroBold }), { load }),
```

`$infer` does not see a difference. The types of the page come from the load. Thus the arms are free. The arms do not have to be different files. The schedule of a component rides its **import attribute**. Thus two bindings of ONE file are two different pages. That answers a real question as a real experiment: does full hydration beat islands?

```ts
import Lab from './Lab.svelte';                          // pure server HTML — zero JS
import LabLive from './Lab.svelte' with { wake: 'load' }; // the SAME file as one live island

export const csrMode = flag('csr-mode', { static: 50, hydrated: 50 });

'/lab': page(csrMode.pick({ static: Lab, hydrated: LabLive }), { load }),
```

**A value** (you have `c`, so pass it and get the answer):

```ts
const trialDays = pricing.pick(c, { control: 7, generous: 30 });    // 7 | 30, typed + total
const label     = hero.pick(c, { control: 'Buy now', bold: 'BUY →' });
const cfg        = hero.pick(c, {
  control: { columns: 2, video: false },
  bold:    { columns: 1, video: true },
});
```

TypeScript makes the map cover each variant. The return type is the union of its values. `pick` pays at 3 or more variants. For a boolean flag, call it. For two variants, a ternary is sufficient.

## Who is the visitor?

Stickiness needs an identity. Declare it **one time**, on the route table:

```ts
export const app = routes(table, {
  visitor: (c) => session_from(c.cookies),   // → { sub: 'u123', … } or undefined
});
```

That becomes [`c.visitor`](/docs/app/server-router) in each place. Flags stick on `c.visitor.sub`. It is also the identity that [mounts sign onward](/docs/app/federation) to other teams. Outside the router, or for anonymous traffic, the fallback is an `og-vid` cookie, then anonymous. **Anonymous visitors always see control.** Thus a split never changes for a visitor you cannot recognize.

For sticky rollouts on **logged-out traffic**, make the cookie:

```ts
import { anonymousVisitor } from 'ogygia/router';

const anon = anonymousVisitor();                       // { cookie?, days? }
routes(table, { visitor: (c) => session(c) ?? anon(c) });
```

## Vendor values — `.value()`

A flag can carry a vendor-authored payload: config edited in the dashboard of the vendor, not in code. Validate it with a schema. An invalid payload is **ignored**. It never enters. That is the same trust-boundary law as for a body schema:

```ts
import * as v from 'valibot';

export const banner = flag('promo-banner', 0, {
  value: v.object({ text: v.string(), tone: v.picklist(['info', 'warn']) }),
  fallback: { text: '', tone: 'info' },   // when the vendor is silent or the value is invalid
});

banner.value(c);   // typed payload — never undefined
```

## OpenFeature — `decide({ source })`

Native flags need no setup. To decide flags with a vendor, connect it **one time**. ogygia depends on no vendor SDK. You give `decide` your own OpenFeature client:

```ts title=hooks.server.ts
import { OpenFeature } from '@openfeature/server-sdk';
import { LaunchDarklyProvider } from '@launchdarkly/openfeature-node-server';
import { decide } from 'ogygia/flag';
import { openfeature } from 'ogygia/flag/openfeature';

OpenFeature.setProvider(new LaunchDarklyProvider(env.LD_KEY));
decide({ source: openfeature(OpenFeature.getClient()) });
```

The source resolves **one time per request**, over exactly the flags that your app declares. After that, each read stays sync. From that instant, a vendor-decided flag and a native one look the same: same calls, same `pick`, same [federation carry](#one-world-across-teams). Decisions are validated against your declared variants. A vendor typo cannot invent a bucket. If the vendor is slow or down, each flag degrades to its native rule. It never gates the first byte.

No vendor SDK? Speak the OpenFeature remote protocol directly:

```ts
import { ofrep } from 'ogygia/flag/openfeature';
decide({ source: ofrep({ url: 'http://flagd:8016' }) });
```

Custom logic or a database? A source is only a function:

```ts
decide({ source: async (queries, c) => {
  const rows = await db.flags(c.visitor?.sub);
  return Object.fromEntries(rows.map((r) => [r.name, r.variant]));
}});
```

> On plain SvelteKit (no ogygia router), `import` your flags module in `hooks.server.ts`. Then each flag is registered before the first request primes the source. Router apps import all route modules at start. Thus their registry is always complete.

## The precedence chain

Each read resolves in this order. The first hit wins:

**`?og-exp` override → carried claims → the source → the native rule → control.**

- **override** — a QA force from the URL (below).
- **carried claims** — a decision that an upstream shell already made (federation).
- **source** — the vendor, if `decide({ source })` set one.
- **native rule** — your targeting function, weights, or rollout.
- **control** — the first variant (or `off`).

## QA override

Force a variant from the URL. It wins over everything, stickiness included:

```
/lab?og-exp=csr-mode:hydrated
/?og-exp=hero:bold,new-checkout:on
```

**Dev-only by default.** In production, an open URL override would let a visitor force unfinished features on, and pollute your data. Thus production ignores `?og-exp` out of the box. Permit QA with a signal that a visitor cannot forge:

```ts title=hooks.server.ts
decide({ overrides: (c) => c.visitor?.roles?.includes('qa') ?? false });
```

## Mutual exclusion — the `layer` option

**The problem.** Two experiments touch the hero section: a redesign, and an autoplay video. If one visitor lands in BOTH treatments and conversions move, you cannot say which change did it. The two measurements contaminate each other. Experiments on the same surface must never share a visitor.

**The mechanism: lanes.** Flags that share a `layer` string form a group:

```ts
const heroRedesign = flag('hero-redesign', { control: 50, bold: 50 }, { layer: 'hero-surface' });
const heroVideo    = flag('hero-video',    { control: 50, video: 50 }, { layer: 'hero-surface' });
```

For each visitor, the layer makes ONE sticky decision, before the logic of a member runs. It hashes `(layer name, visitor id)` over the **sorted** member names. That selects the **owner** for the visitor. Think of all traffic in N equal lanes, one lane per member. A read of a member flag then starts with one question: *am I the owner for this visitor?*

- **Not the owner** → answer control, immediately. The targeting and the weights of the flag never run for that visitor.
- **The owner** → continue as if the layer did not exist, inside your own lane.

Walkthrough: visitor `puru` hashes into the lane of `hero-video`. `heroRedesign(puru)` → `'control'`, always. Its 50/50 never executes. `heroVideo(puru)` → its own 50/50. The same inputs hash the same way on each request and on each replica. The names are sorted, so module import order can never change a lane. There is no storage, no cross-server coordination, and nothing to sync.

**The arithmetic to remember.** A member only experiments inside its own lane. Thus its real exposure is its split **divided by the member count**. A 50% variant in a 2-member layer reaches 25% of all traffic. Add a third member, and each lane shrinks to a third.

**What outranks the layer.** The override, carried claims, and the vendor source all sit ABOVE the lane gate in the precedence chain. `?og-exp=hero-redesign:bold` shows QA the variant, also for a visitor that the other member owns. A carried decision from an upstream shell is applied as-is. A vendor that answers for a flag bypasses the lane. The vendor then owns exclusion. Do not run the same surface in both systems.

## Measurement — `decide({ exposure })`

ogygia ships **no metrics pipeline**. The decision is the primitive. The count is the job of your analytics. The one seam is an exposure sink. It fires one time per request per flag, on the first read. Under SSR, the decision *is* the render of the variant. Batching is built in:

```ts title=hooks.server.ts
decide({
  exposure: (events) => analytics.track(events),   // an array of { name, variant, sub, at }
  batch: { max: 25, ms: 2000 },                     // flushed at the cap, the timer, or request end
});
```

A sink that throws never breaks a request. The tail drains at the end of each request (serverless-safe). For logs and `data-og-exp` stamps, `stamp(c)` gives `name:variant`:

```svelte
<section data-og-exp={data.stamp}>…</section>
```

## One world across teams

"Carried" in the precedence chain is the federation tier. Each flag that a request **decides** registers its bucket itself. When a shell [mounts the apps of other teams](/docs/app/federation), the full world of the visitor travels in the signed claims. No person lists anything by hand. A forgotten entry cannot silently fork a visitor between teams. A carried decision beats the local native rule. Thus one visitor never sits in two worlds.

A shell that only **routes** to teams, and never reads the flag itself, pre-decides it on the table. The bucket then still travels:

```ts
routes(table, { visitor: session, flags: [csrMode] });   // decide-but-don't-render
```

## The flag manifest

Each production build writes an inventory of the `flag()` call sites that it compiled. The inventory is AST-resolved: renamed and namespace imports count; an unrelated local function named `flag` does not. It goes to:

```
node_modules/.ogygia/flags-manifest.json   →  { flags: [{ name, kind, file, line }], names: [...] }
```

Flags rot. A code-first setup gets most of the industry flag-debt tools from the inventory alone. Record the manifest in CI. Diff it per release. Alarm on a name that lives past its retirement date. The `ogygia_flags` MCP tool reads it live. The retirement of a flag stays a human edit: delete the flag, keep the branch that won. The manifest tells you which flags are overdue.
